      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: BASIC CONCEPT OF SOIL"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Alluvial soil is the most extensive and fertile soil type in India, formed primarily by river deposits in the Northern Plains and coastal deltas.",
        "Black soil (Regur) is rich in clay, iron, calcium, and magnesium, possessing high water-retention capacity which makes it ideal for cotton cultivation.",
        "Laterite soil forms in tropical areas with high rainfall and temperature through the process of intense leaching of silica.",
        "Saline and Alkaline soils are commonly referred to as Usar soils and contain high concentrations of sodium, potassium, and magnesium.",
        "Soil conservation strategies include terrace farming, contour ploughing, strip cropping, and establishing shelterbelts to prevent erosion."
      ],
      "id": "kn1gz4g"
    },
